  "body": "https://whatpr.org/html/3934/rendering.html#the-fieldset-and-legend-elements\r\n> The child's used value of '\\-webkit\\-appearance' is 'fieldset\\-legend'\\.\r\n\r\nUsing `-webkit-appearance` to trigger the special _rendered legend_ layout has the side-effect that it resets the themed appearance that some form controls have by default.  For example,\r\n```html\r\n<fieldset>\r\n  <input type=button style=\"-webkit-appearance:fieldset-legend\" value=\"button\">\r\n  <input type=button value=\"button\">\r\n</fieldset>\r\n```\r\nrenders as (screenshot from my prototype [implementation](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1483787) of `-webkit-appearance:fieldset/fieldset-legend` in Gecko):\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/4010828/44731529-a5c13180-aae3-11e8-95b4-dd62ad094621.png)\r\n\r\n\r\nI think that some of the HTML form control elements could be useful as a _rendered legend_ so I think `-webkit-appearance` is not the right property to use for this. I think it would be better to mint a new CSS property to trigger the _rendered legend_ layout, something like `legend:auto|none`. Then there is no risk of it affecting the default appearance of the element the author chooses to use as the _rendered legend_.\r\n",
